From: Joel Gerber <Jitsusama@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] service/notifications: send an activation token before an action

Clicking a notification's action is meant to bring the program's window
forward, but Wayland compositors refuse to let a program raise itself
without an activation token tied to recent input. Version 1.2 of the
notifications specification has the server send one through the
ActivationToken signal just before ActionInvoked, which the interface
already declares but the server never emitted, so programs heard the
click and couldn't act on it.

core gains ActivationTokens, through which code that knows nothing of the
windowing system asks for a token, as the platform menu hook lets the
Wayland plugin shape menus. The Wayland plugin answers it with
xdg_activation_v1, using the serial, seat and window of the last input
Qt saw, which is what compositors check. An invoked action now asks for a
token, then sends ActivationToken and ActionInvoked, and closes the
notification as before. With no handler, or no xdg_activation_v1 global,
no token is sent and the action is invoked as it was.

The shell's QML asks for one too, with Quickshell.activationToken(callback),
for when another process serves notifications for the shell: the shell
asks for the token as the click lands and hands it to that server with the
action chosen, since a process with no window can't ask the compositor
itself.

---
